([PROBLEM 2: Design and Construction of a Diagonal Scale]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem Statement: On a railway map, an actual distance of 36 km is represented by a 9) Tj
T*
(cm line. Construct a diagonal scale to read kilometers, hectometers, and decameters, up) Tj
T*
(to a maximum distance of 60 km. Mark a distance of 43.6 km \(or 43 km and 6 hm\).) Tj
T*
(Mathematical Derivation & Construction Steps:) Tj
T*
(* Step 1: Calculate Representative Fraction \(RF\):) Tj
T*
(- RF = Drawing length / Actual length = 9 cm / \(36 km x 1000 m/km x 100 cm/m\) = 9 /) Tj
T*
(3,600,000 = 1 / 400,000.) Tj
T*
(* Step 2: Calculate Length of Scale \(LOS\):) Tj
T*
(- LOS = RF x ML = \(1 / 400,000\) x 60 km = \(1 / 400,000\) x 6,000,000 cm = 15 cm.) Tj
T*
(* Step 3: Construction of Scale Outline & Main Divisions:) Tj
T*
(- Draw a rectangle of length 15 cm and height 5 cm.) Tj
T*
(- Divide the 15 cm length into 6 equal main divisions. Each division represents 10 km) Tj
T*
(\(15 cm / 6 = 2.5 cm per 10 km\).) Tj
T*
(- Mark 0 at the end of the first division, and label 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 km to the right.) Tj
T*
(* Step 4: Division of Secondary Unit \(Kilometers\):) Tj
T*
(- Divide the first 10-km division on the left into 10 equal parts. Each part represents) Tj
T*
(1 km.) Tj
T*
(* Step 5: Diagonal Principle for Tertiary Unit \(Hectometers\):) Tj
T*
(- Divide the vertical height of 5 cm into 10 equal parts \(numbered 0 to 10 upwards\).) Tj
T*
(Each vertical division represents 1 Hectometer \(1 hm = 0.1 km\).) Tj
T*
(- Connect the 0 mark on top to the 1 mark on bottom, creating diagonals of slope.) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
